  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  A moderate winter system is on the way for today that is\n   starting to cause increasing concerns for travel.\n\n- Another moderate system is on the way for Friday night and\n  Saturday that will likely cause moderate travel impacts in the\n  region.\n\nThe remnants of an atmospheric river is knocking on the door to \nthe Northern Rockies tonight. This moisture will push into the \nregion this morning causing widespread precipitation. Many \nobservation sites just to the west have been slowly warming \novernight so snow levels are still expected to rise throughout the\nday. Most locations will start off as snow, but then the valleys \nare expected to transition over to rain as snow levels rise to \naround 6000 feet by this afternoon. Lolo, Lost Trail, Lookout and \nMarias passes are expected to receive a couple of inches of new \nsnow during this event potentially causing minor to moderate \nimpacts to the roads. \n\nA secondary disturbance is still on track to pass through the\nregion Friday night into Saturday morning. The bulk of the\nprecipitation is anticipated to be south of the Highway 200\ncorridor. Snow levels will drop to around 3500 feet by Saturday\nmorning. This disturbance has a better chance of bringing moderate\nsnow impacts to the mountain passes and around the Butte/Anaconda\nregion by Saturday.\n\nA weak ridge builds over the region Saturday into Sunday causing a\ndrying trend for the remainder of the holiday travel weekend. Then\nthe long term models indicate that the Northern Rockies will\nreturn to an active weather pattern as more weak disturbances make\ntheir way through the region.",
